---
description: Keep local PAW and llama.cpp inference strictly single-threaded
alwaysApply: true
---

# Single-Thread Local PAW Inference

- Never run two local PAW/llama.cpp inference calls concurrently in one process.
  The interpreter shares mutable model and KV-cache state; concurrency can cause
  severe slowdown, corruption, aborts, or segmentation faults.
- Use exactly one supervised process-global inference subprocess for function
  execution and warmup. Do not add per-rule, per-program, or per-request
  inference pools.
- PAW compilation may use a separate executor because compilation is remote and
  must not be blocked behind local inference.
- Function creation, interpreter loading, warmup, and inference must respect the
  same serialization boundary.
- A thread timeout does not stop a native call. On timeout, terminate the entire
  inference subprocess before starting its replacement.
- Keep a regression test that starts multiple callers and proves maximum active
  local inference concurrency is exactly one.
